  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
    <a href="/districts/new-rochelle-city-sd/">← Back to New Rochelle City SD Hub</a>
</div>

<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">New Rochelle City SD Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in New Rochelle City SD, New Rochelle.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>Special Education Office</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 District Office:</strong><br>New Rochelle City SD</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:914-576-4268">914-576-4268</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📍 Mailing Address:</strong><br>515 North Avenue, New Rochelle, NY 10801</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYSED Special Education QA (SEQA).
    </div>

    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                    <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common District Pitfalls & Advocacy Tips for New Rochelle Parents
        </h2>

        <div class="pitfall-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(350px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="pitfall-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Delayed Evaluations</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Districts sometimes delay initial evaluations beyond the legally mandated 60-day timeline (New York State Part 200.4(a)). This can significantly impact a child's access to timely support.
                </p>
                <strong style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #333;">Advocacy Tip:</strong>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    From the moment you suspect your child needs special education services, send a formal, written request for an evaluation via certified mail. Track the 60-day timeline meticulously. If the evaluation is not completed within the timeframe, immediately file a written complaint with the district's special education director and consider contacting NYSED. Document everything, including dates of communication and any justifications provided for the delay. Citing the specific regulation (200.4(a)) in your communication can be helpful.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pitfall-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Predetermined IEPs</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    A predetermined IEP occurs when the IEP is written before the meeting, without genuine input from the parent and other IEP team members. This violates the spirit and letter of the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).
                </p>
                <strong style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #333;">Advocacy Tip:</strong>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    Actively participate in the IEP meeting. Bring your own data, observations, and proposed goals. If you feel the IEP is already decided, politely but firmly state your concerns on the record (and in writing afterward). Request that your dissenting opinion be noted in the IEP. If the predetermination is blatant, consider requesting another IEP meeting with a different facilitator or escalating to the district's special education director. Remind the team that IDEA requires meaningful parent participation.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pitfall-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Inadequate Related Services</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Sometimes, districts may not provide the frequency, duration, or type of related services (e.g., speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling) necessary for a child to make meaningful progress. This can also manifest as unqualified providers delivering related services.
                </p>
                <strong style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #333;">Advocacy Tip:</strong>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    Gather data to support your request for increased or different related services. This might include progress reports from current therapists, independent evaluations, or observations of your child's needs. Research best practices for your child's specific disability. If the district refuses to provide adequate services, request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense (if you disagree with the district's evaluation). If the IEE supports your request, the district must consider its findings. You can cite IDEA's requirements for related services (34 CFR §300.34) and the right to an IEE (34 CFR §300.502) in your communication.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="pitfall-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">"Stay Put" Rights Misinterpreted</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                     "Stay Put" (34 CFR §300.518) protects a student's current educational placement during a dispute. Districts may try to circumvent this by claiming a change is minor or doesn't trigger Stay Put.
                </p>
                <strong style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #333;">Advocacy Tip:</strong>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    If you disagree with a proposed change to your child's IEP and request mediation or a due process hearing, immediately invoke "Stay Put" in writing. Clearly state that you want your child to remain in their current placement while the dispute is resolved. Be prepared to advocate strongly for this right, as it can be crucial for maintaining stability for your child. Consult with a special education attorney if the district resists honoring Stay Put.
                </p>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions for New Rochelle Parents
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px;">

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">How do I request an evaluation for special education services in New Rochelle City SD?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    Send a written request (email or letter) to the district's special education office (contact information above). The letter should include your child's name, date of birth, grade, school, and a brief explanation of your concerns. Clearly state that you are requesting an evaluation to determine eligibility for special education services. Remember to keep a copy for your records.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are my rights if I disagree with the IEP developed by the CSE?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    You have several rights if you disagree with the IEP, including the right to request mediation, file a State Complaint with NYSED, or request a Due Process Hearing. Mediation is a voluntary process where a neutral third party helps you and the district reach an agreement. A State Complaint is a formal written complaint to NYSED alleging that the district has violated IDEA. A Due Process Hearing is a more formal legal process where an impartial hearing officer makes a decision about the dispute. You should consult with a special education attorney or advocate to understand your options and choose the best course of action.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an IEE, and how do I request one?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    An Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) is an evaluation of your child conducted by a qualified professional who is not employed by the school district. You have the right to request an IEE at public expense if you disagree with an evaluation conducted by the district. To request an IEE, send a written request to the district's special education office, stating that you disagree with their evaluation and are requesting an IEE at public expense. The district must either grant your request or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate.
                </p>
            </div>

             <div class="faq-item">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Where can I find more information about special education rights in New York State?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
                    You can find information on the New York State Education Department (NYSED) website (http://www.nysed.gov/specialed). Also, consider contacting organizations like the Parent to Parent of New York State or the Disability Rights New York. It's always recommended to consult with a qualified special education attorney to fully understand your rights.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

</div>
</main>
